  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge is over the central part of the country \nextending from Texas to North Dakota. An upper trough is centered \nover the Pacific Northwest with another upper trough over the \nnortheast part of the country. Winds across south central and \ncentral Nebraska and north central Kansas are generally out of the \nsouth to southeast at around 5 to 15 MPH with gusts as high as 20 \nMPH. High temperatures today will generally range from the low 90s \nto the upper 90s. Heat index values will generally range from the \nmid 90s to around 106 degrees. A Heat Advisory remains in effect \nuntil 8 PM this evening for north central Kansas and portions of \nsouth central and central Nebraska. Low temperatures tonight will \nrange from the mid 60s to mid 70s. Some showers and storms may move \ninto/develop across north central Kansas this afternoon. Otherwise, \nshowers and storms may develop (up to around a 50% chance) or move \ninto western portions of the forecast area later tonight (beginning \naround 4 AM). There is a low risk of strong to marginally severe \nstorms this afternoon into this evening and again tonight with hail \nup to quarter size and wind gusts up to around 60 MPH.\n\nAdditional development and eastward expansion of showers and storms \nis expected Sunday morning and afternoon. Additional showers and \nstorms may again develop Sunday evening across portions of the area. \nHigh temperatures on Sunday are expected to be slightly cooler (by \naround 2-3 degrees) than today with upper level heights decreasing \nslightly. Heat index values on Sunday are expected to range from the \nlower 90s to around 102 degrees. South to southeast winds are \nexpected at around 5 to 15 MPH with gusts as high as 20 to 30 MPH \nduring the afternoon. Low temperatures tomorrow night will be \nsimilar to the previous night. Temperatures will warm up a little on \nMonday from the previous day as a surface trough strengthens across \nthe area. Highs on Monday are expected to range from the low 90s to \nnear 100 degrees. Heat index values are expected to be in the mid \n90s to around 104 degrees. Winds will increase Monday afternoon out \nof the south to around 15 to 30 MPH with gusts up to near 40 MPH in \nsome areas. Low temperatures Monday night (upper 60s to upper 70s) \nwill be warmer with mostly cloudy skies and higher wind speeds. \nShowers and storms may impact parts of the area beginning Monday \nevening/night in advance of a cold front (up to a 60% chance). Some \nof these storms may become strong to severe. \n\nA cold front is expected to move into the area on Tuesday with high \ntemperatures ranging from the mid 80s to around 101 degrees. Heat \nindex values from around Highway 6 and southward may range from \naround 100 to 105 degrees. Low temperatures Tuesday night are \nexpected to range from the low 60s to the low/mid 70s. A surface \nhigh will be across the area on Wednesday with high temperatures in \nthe low 80s to low 90s. Highs on Thursday will be similar to those \non Wednesday. Temperatures will warm up some on Friday as a surface \ntrough strengthens across the area. Shower and storm chances \ncontinue Tuesday through Friday with the highest chances Tuesday \ninto Wednesday (up to 70% chance).",
